answer.text:
Assuming the ring oscillator’s supply current is dominated by charging the stage capacitances:

- For an \(N\)-stage ring,
  \[
  P \approx N\,C_{\text{eff}}\,V_{DD}^{2}\,f_{osc}
  \]
  so the average supply current is
  \[
  I_{DD}=\frac{P}{V_{DD}} \approx N\,C_{\text{eff}}\,V_{DD}\,f_{osc}.
  \]

- Hence the small-signal conductance seen at the supply node is
  \[
  g_x=\frac{d I_{DD}}{d V_{DD}}
     =N\,C_{\text{eff}}\!\left(f_{osc}+V_{DD}\frac{d f_{osc}}{dV_{DD}}\right).
  \]

So the small-signal resistance is

\[
\boxed{
R_x=\left(\frac{d I_{DD}}{d V_{DD}}\right)^{-1}
=\frac{1}{N\,C_{\text{eff}}\!\left(f_{osc}+V_{DD}\frac{d f_{osc}}{dV_{DD}}\right)}
}
\]

For the **3-stage** ring oscillator in the figure:

\[
\boxed{
R_x=\frac{1}{3\,C_{\text{eff}}\!\left(f_{osc}+V_{DD}\frac{d f_{osc}}{dV_{DD}}\right)}
}
\]

If you ignore the dependence of oscillation frequency on supply voltage, this reduces to

\[
\boxed{
R_x \approx \frac{1}{3\,C_{\text{eff}}\,f_{osc}}
}
\]

where \(C_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective switched capacitance per stage.
